{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>On the morrow after the sabbath.</b> Our Sages, of blessed memory, said: on the morrow after the festival [first day of Passover]. The deniers said it is as it sounds [the morrow after the weekly Sabbath]. The believers brought proofs from the sabbatical year and the Jubilee, and the great fast [Yom Kippur], and the day of sounding [Rosh HaShanah], concerning which 'a solemn rest' is written, and likewise 'a solemn rest' is said of the first day of Sukkot and of the eighth day. And they said that 'seven sabbaths' means 'seven weeks,' and similarly באי השבת (when the Sabbath comes) together with יוצאי השבת (those who go out from the Sabbath) — and these are two interpretations in a single verse, and behold thirty investigations [are needed], and their proofs are complete. A sage in Rome brought a proof from 'the morrow after the Passover: unleavened cakes and parched grain' [Joshua 5:11], and he did not know that it endangers his own soul, for the Passover [sacrifice] is on the fourteenth day, and its morrow is the fifteenth day. And similarly it is written, 'And they journeyed from Rameses in the first month' [Numbers 33:3]. Now to eat parched grain is forbidden until the waving of the omer has passed. The Gaon [Saadia] said that there are two Passovers: the Passover of YHWH and the Passover of Israel, and the Passover of YHWH is on the night of the fifteenth, and so the morrow of the Passover in Joshua is the sixteenth day. And the meaning of 'the morrow of the Passover' written in the Torah would be 'the morrow of the slaughter of the Passover [sacrifice].' But he said nothing, for the festival is not called Passover except because YHWH passed over the houses, and its morrow is the morning of the fifteenth day, and similarly it is written, 'all that day, and all that night, and all the next day' [Joshua 10:13]. The Gaon also said that the unleavened cakes and parched grain were from the old [produce], and this is not far-fetched, for Scripture says concerning the waving of the omer that it shall be 'from the beginning of the sickle in the standing grain' — and if the waving of the omer was on the fifteenth day, when did they reap and make unleavened cakes? Moreover, there is no reaping, for it is 'the first of the barley harvest'; also there is something like a proof for his interpretation, for he said 'from the produce of the land' [Joshua 5:11], and it is possible that this name is applied to grain that belongs to the previous year. And the meaning of 'the produce of the land' is the land of Sihon and Og, and the proof is that afterward it says 'the produce of the land of Canaan' [Joshua 5:12]. Moreover, קלוי (parched) is not like קלי (parched grain), and Scripture says, 'you shall eat neither bread nor parched grain nor fresh ears' [Leviticus 23:14]. And the verse 'parched with fire' is no argument, for it is a description of the action — for 'fresh ears parched with fire' is called קלוי. Moreover, one must answer: from where do we know that the priest waved the omer offering after crossing the Jordan, for Scripture says, 'which I give you' — and further, it had not yet been given to them? And Scripture did not come to mention unleavened cakes and parched grain except because the manna ceased. And so [one must] interpret the whole verse. The Gaon further said: if the Sabbath is as it sounds [a weekly Sabbath], from which Sabbath do we begin to count? And Yeshu'ah [ben Judah] answered that eighteen days are those on which an offering is brought, and they are called appointed times, apart from the weekly Sabbaths, and since an offering is brought on the day of the waving of the omer as a sign and witness, therefore the waving is on one of the days of the festival of spring. But behold, he forgot the new-moon offerings that were not mentioned. Moreover, Scripture says, 'Thus you shall do for the seven-day festival,' and it should have said, 'apart from the waving of the omer.' This too is no argument against us, for we rely in commandments upon tradition. And so on the day of Shavuot he did not mention in the Pinchas passage anything besides the lambs of well-being, whereas above [it says] one bull, two rams, and in Pinchas two bulls, one ram. Also, the believer can answer that by way of prophecy the author knew that there would be in it the waving of the omer, that the day of the Sabbath would be the morrow of the festival. And the proof is the arrangement of the bread [showbread] that was on the Sabbath, and Scripture mentions how to count in the first year, and then they offered well-being offerings and the burnt offering mentioned above, and afterward that mentioned in Pinchas. And after that he said, 'your weeks — seven weeks,' and did not say 'Sabbaths'; and it is no proof, for the beginning of the week is the first day, and 'complete shall they be' [Leviticus 23:15] — two weeks will prove. And behold, I will hint to you one sod: all the appointed times depend on a day known from the new moon, and in the festival of Weeks no number [of a day of the month] is stated, because of the counting, which is a commandment. And our Sages, of blessed memory, transmitted that on the festival of Weeks the Torah was given, and concerning it is it said: 'a festival of YHWH for us.'" "Some say that every instance of <b>for your acceptance</b> means 'that it be for you for acceptance' — 'and he waved the omer before YHWH for your acceptance' — and its meaning is that you shall wave willingly, and the reason [for the lamb] is that you shall give a lamb of the first year, so that you may be accepted."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The text refers to multiple named disputants: 'the Gaon' is resolved as Saadia per standard convention; 'Yeshu'ah' is Yeshu'ah ben Judah (Abu al-Faraj Furkan ibn Asad), the 11th-century Karaite exegete. 'The deniers' and 'the believers' refer to Karaites and Rabbanites respectively regarding the count of the omer. Several passages are textually compressed with run-on arguments; translated as written without structural clarification."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:12:13.885889967Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}